## 1. Objective and scope

Determine why nominally identical fused-filament-fabrication polymer tensile coupons exhibit persistent differences in tensile strength by:

- Estimating causal effects of controllable manufacturing and testing factors.
- Separating systematic effects from batch, machine, operator, specimen, and measurement variation.
- Identifying measurable mediators—such as density, void structure, crystallinity, geometry, and interlayer bonding—that account for observed effects.
- Discriminating among competing explanations:
  1. feedstock moisture or lot variability;
  2. thermal-history differences;
  3. extrusion-flow or dimensional variation;
  4. orientation, raster, and interlayer-bond effects;
  5. cooling and environmental effects;
  6. post-print aging or conditioning;
  7. tensile-test alignment, grip, strain-rate, or metrology artifacts.

The primary outcome is tensile strength computed from independently measured load and gauge-section area. Secondary outcomes include elastic modulus, yield stress, strain at break, fracture location, and failure mode. Conclusions apply to the studied polymer, coupon geometry, process window, machines, and test laboratory; extrapolation beyond these conditions requires confirmation.

## 2. Assumptions

- Coupons labeled “identical” share nominal CAD geometry, slicer settings, material grade, print orientation, and test procedure, but may differ in measured process history.
- The tensile machine, dimensional metrology, environmental sensors, and relevant printer sensors can be calibrated or their measurement error quantified.
- Experimental interventions can be randomized without unacceptable safety or equipment constraints.
- Stable-unit treatment value is approximately valid: one coupon’s treatment does not affect another except through shared machine state, chamber environment, or material handling. Such shared states will be recorded and controlled through blocking or whole-plot assignment.
- Printer history, nozzle wear, maintenance, and material exposure may create carryover; washout/purge procedures and treatment-order randomization will address this.
- Destructive testing prevents repeated tensile measurements on the same coupon. Replication therefore occurs through independently printed specimens.
- No single measured mediator is assumed to be the mechanism in advance.
- Missingness and failed prints may be treatment-dependent; all exclusions and failure reasons will be retained and analyzed.

A pre-study causal directed acyclic graph will represent:

- **Treatments:** moisture, material lot, nozzle temperature, bed/chamber conditions, cooling, speed, commanded flow, orientation/raster, machine, nozzle state, conditioning, test rate, alignment, and operator.
- **Mediators:** actual thermal history, extrusion mass, geometry, porosity, crystallinity, residual stress, surface condition, and bond quality.
- **Outcomes:** tensile strength and associated mechanical properties.
- **Potential confounders:** time, ambient conditions, printer maintenance, spool order, machine drift, and tester drift.

Variables downstream of assigned treatments will not be adjusted for when estimating total effects; they will be used only in explicitly defined mediation analyses.

## 3. Research design

### Phase A: Reproducibility audit and baseline decomposition

Freeze the CAD file, slicer version, nominal settings, toolpath file, polymer grade, conditioning procedure, and tensile standard. Print a balanced baseline set across:

- multiple printers, including repeated runs on each printer;
- at least two nozzles or nozzle-life states where feasible;
- multiple material lots and spools;
- multiple days and time blocks;
- randomized coupon positions on the build plate;
- randomized print and tensile-test order;
- multiple trained operators, with operator assignments randomized where feasible.

Use a nested/crossed design that permits estimates of variation attributable to machine, run, day, spool/lot, build position, operator, coupon, and tensile-test batch. Print more than one coupon per build so within-build and between-build variability can be distinguished.

Before destructive testing, randomly assign coupon identifiers and blind tensile-test personnel to manufacturing conditions. Randomize test order within blocks to separate specimen effects from tester drift.

### Phase B: Randomized screening experiment

Intervene on plausible causes rather than relying only on observational correlations. Use a blocked fractional-factorial or definitive-screening design representing realistic low and high values for factors such as:

- dry versus controlled moisture exposure;
- material lot or spool;
- nozzle temperature;
- bed and chamber temperature;
- cooling level;
- print speed or volumetric flow demand;
- commanded flow multiplier;
- layer height;
- raster/orientation strategy;
- controlled versus variable post-print conditioning;
- nozzle wear or verified new versus used nozzle;
- tensile-test strain rate;
- deliberate, bounded alignment or gripping differences.

Treat printer, day, and build as blocks or whole plots when factors are difficult to change at coupon level. Randomize all factors at the lowest feasible assignment level. Balance factor combinations across machines, time, plate positions, and tensile-test batches.

Include center points and repeat baseline controls throughout the schedule to detect curvature and temporal drift. Preserve specimens from each condition for non-destructive or companion-specimen characterization.

### Phase C: Focused causal confirmation

Advance factors showing meaningful effects or strong interactions into a fully crossed randomized experiment. Include at least:

- the implicated factor;
- major interacting factors;
- an untreated control;
- sufficient independent builds, not merely multiple coupons from one build;
- replication across machines, lots, and days.

Select levels that cover the operating region without intentionally producing grossly defective parts. Estimate dose-response where the factor is continuous.

### Phase D: Mechanism and test-artifact discrimination

For confirmed manufacturing causes, randomize the causal factor and measure candidate mediators before testing. Where practical, independently manipulate the proposed mediator or use a second intervention expected to affect the same mediator through a different route.

To distinguish specimen differences from testing artifacts:

- test randomized coupons on more than one calibrated tensile frame or fixture;
- use independent strain measurement, such as an extensometer and a validated optical method;
- verify machine load using traceable standards;
- quantify alignment and bending;
- remeasure cross-sectional area independently and blindly;
- rotate operators and test order;
- use reference specimens with well-characterized, stable properties.

If apparent strength differences follow the test frame, operator, area method, or specimen position rather than the production treatment, classify the dominant explanation as metrological or procedural.

### Competing-explanation predictions

- **Moisture:** strength differences track measured water content or exposure history; drying changes outcomes; effects may include characteristic voids, hydrolytic degradation, or ductility changes.
- **Thermal history/interlayer bonding:** outcomes track measured extrusion/chamber temperature histories and orientation; fractures localize at bead interfaces; interactions with cooling and speed are expected.
- **Under-/over-extrusion and geometry:** outcomes track coupon mass, local area, bead dimensions, and porosity; effects shrink when stress uses accurate local area but persist if internal defects remain.
- **Material-lot chemistry or morphology:** differences remain after matching process settings and conditioning, follow randomized lot assignment, and correspond to molecular, thermal, or crystallinity measures.
- **Aging/conditioning:** effects follow time, humidity, and temperature after printing and are altered by standardized conditioning.
- **Testing artifact:** indicated differences change with frame, fixture, alignment, grip, operator, test order, or area-measurement method; reference specimens exhibit parallel shifts.
- **Unmeasured machine state:** persistent printer or run effects remain after measured covariates, and transfer with hardware components, maintenance state, or calibration rather than material.

## 4. Data or evidence needed

For every coupon, retain a complete chain-of-custody record linking:

- raw-material lot, spool, storage, drying, handling, and exposure history;
- printer, nozzle identity/material/diameter, nozzle lifetime, maintenance, and calibration;
- CAD, slicer, toolpath, firmware, and all commanded settings;
- print start time, build location, neighboring specimens, run order, and interruptions;
- environmental and process sensor time series;
- post-print storage, age, temperature, and humidity;
- dimensional and structural characterization;
- tensile machine, fixture, grips, operator, calibration status, test order, and raw force/displacement/strain data;
- deviations, failed prints, exclusions, and missing measurements.

Evidence should include:

- repeated baseline controls dispersed through the study;
- calibration and gauge repeatability/reproducibility records;
- sufficient independent builds at each condition;
- machine-, lot-, and day-level replication;
- raw sensor streams and unprocessed mechanical-test curves;
- fracture images and adjudicated failure classifications;
- prespecified evidence thresholds and analysis code specifications prepared before outcome unblinding.

Conduct an a priori simulation-based power analysis using baseline variance components. Choose replication to detect the smallest practically important change in tensile strength and key interactions while accounting for clustering within builds. Do not count multiple coupons from one build as equivalent to independent builds.

## 5. Measurement

### Outcomes

Measure:

- ultimate tensile strength using local gauge-section area;
- peak load as an area-independent companion outcome;
- elastic modulus;
- yield stress under a prespecified definition;
- strain at break;
- toughness;
- fracture location and mode;
- incidence of grip failures or tests invalid under the chosen standard.

Retain force, displacement, extensometer/optical strain, and acquisition timestamps at full practical resolution.

### Manufacturing exposures

Measure actual, not only commanded:

- filament moisture immediately before printing and after relevant exposure;
- filament diameter and ovality along the used segment;
- filament mass consumed and coupon mass;
- nozzle, melt-zone, bed, chamber, and ambient temperature histories;
- humidity;
- print duration, speed, acceleration, cooling command, and fan performance;
- extrusion pressure or drive load, if available;
- power interruptions, pauses, and anomalous events;
- nozzle geometry and wear before and after assigned runs.

Use independent calibrated sensors for a subset to assess printer telemetry bias.

### Coupon characteristics and candidate mediators

Before destructive testing, measure:

- gauge width and thickness at several prespecified locations;
- local cross-sectional area using a validated method;
- mass and bulk density;
- surface roughness and bead geometry;
- warpage and build orientation;
- porosity, void fraction, void size/orientation, and defect location using non-destructive imaging or matched companion specimens;
- crystallinity and thermal transitions;
- residual stress where feasible;
- molecular degradation indicators where moisture or overheating is plausible;
- interlayer/bond morphology using fractured or companion specimens.

Destructive mediator measurements should use matched coupons produced in the same randomized build position scheme, not fragments selected after the outcome is known.

### Testing-process measurements

Record and verify:

- load-cell and displacement calibration;
- extensometer or optical-strain calibration;
- frame compliance;
- grip pressure, specimen insertion depth, and slippage;
- axial alignment and bending strain;
- test speed and actual strain rate;
- laboratory temperature and humidity;
- operator and test order.

Perform measurement-system analyses for dimensions, moisture, porosity, and tensile outcomes. Use blinded repeat measurements to estimate repeatability, reproducibility, and inter-rater reliability.

## 6. Analysis

### Estimands

Prespecify:

- average total causal effect of each randomized manufacturing or testing factor on tensile strength;
- dose-response for selected continuous factors;
- factor interactions, especially temperature-by-speed, moisture-by-temperature, cooling-by-orientation, and machine-by-setting;
- causal effects on mediators;
- controlled or interventional mediation effects for supported mechanisms;
- variance components attributable to machine, build, day, lot/spool, plate position, operator, and residual coupon variation.

### Primary models

Analyze randomized phases by intention to treat using hierarchical mixed-effects models or equivalent design-based estimators:

- fixed effects for assigned factors, prespecified interactions, blocks, and center-point curvature;
- random effects for printer, build, day, spool, test batch, and other sampled grouping units;
- heteroskedastic residuals if variance changes by treatment;
- cluster-robust or randomization-based uncertainty at the assignment level.

Use peak load and accurately area-normalized stress as co-primary diagnostic outcomes. A factor affecting computed strength but not peak load may indicate geometry or area-measurement mechanisms.

Estimate baseline variance components separately from causal treatment effects. Report effect estimates, uncertainty intervals, standardized effects, absolute changes, and probabilities or confidence that effects exceed practical thresholds—not only significance tests.

### Confounding and time trends

Randomization is the principal confounding control. For unrandomized baseline associations:

- adjust only for prespecified pre-exposure causes of both exposure and outcome;
- model time, maintenance, and environmental trends;
- avoid conditioning on mediators or colliders;
- label resulting estimates as associational unless identification assumptions are defensible.

If protocol deviations occur, retain intention-to-treat estimates and optionally add instrumented or per-protocol analyses with explicit assumptions.

### Mediation and explanation testing

For each confirmed treatment effect:

1. establish that assignment changes the candidate mediator;
2. establish that assignment changes the outcome;
3. estimate mediation using models respecting treatment–mediator–outcome temporal order;
4. assess treatment–mediator interactions;
5. perform sensitivity analysis for unmeasured mediator–outcome confounding;
6. seek independent intervention or replication evidence for the proposed mechanism.

Evidence favoring an explanation requires predicted, treatment-specific mediator changes and outcome changes in the expected direction. Mere correlation between a mediator and strength is insufficient.

### Artifact and robustness analyses

- Compare results across tensile frames, area methods, operators, and strain measurements.
- Test whether reference-specimen outcomes drift with experimental coupons.
- Recalculate outcomes under plausible measurement-error distributions.
- Analyze valid tests and all attempted tests, with test invalidity as an outcome.
- Examine residuals for plate-position, temporal, and spatial patterns.
- Conduct leave-one-machine, leave-one-lot, and leave-one-day-out analyses.
- Correct or hierarchically shrink multiplicity in screening; reserve confirmatory inference for prespecified factors.
- Keep exclusions blinded to treatment/outcome where possible and report analyses with and without disputed observations.

## 7. Decision / stopping rule

Define before data collection:

- a smallest practically important effect in tensile strength;
- acceptable baseline repeatability and measurement-system error;
- minimum numbers of independent builds, machines, lots, and days;
- confirmatory uncertainty and multiplicity criteria;
- criteria for declaring a mechanism supported.

A factor will be considered a **confirmed causal contributor** when:

1. it is randomized or otherwise credibly identified;
2. its effect exceeds the practical threshold with prespecified uncertainty;
3. the effect replicates across independent builds and at least two relevant contexts, such as machine, lot, or day;
4. the result is robust to valid outcome definitions and measurement-error checks.

A mechanism will be considered **supported** when the intervention changes the proposed mediator and outcome as predicted, mediation evidence is robust to stated sensitivity analyses, and an independent manipulation or external replication within the study is concordant.

A testing artifact will be considered **supported** when the discrepancy tracks randomized or instrumented test conditions or area calculations, appears in reference specimens, and does not consistently track randomized manufacturing conditions.

Stop screening only after the prespecified sample size and measurement-quality thresholds are reached. Stop confirmatory sampling when either:

- precision is sufficient to classify all priority factors as practically important or negligible; or
- a prespecified sequential-analysis boundary is crossed.

Pause rather than stop for calibration failure, excessive missing sensor data, uncontrolled process drift, or invalid-test rates above the preset threshold. If substantial unexplained variance remains after confirmed factors are controlled, initiate a new preregistered cycle focused on residual machine, time, spatial, or material signals rather than claiming complete explanation.

## 8. Limitations

- Results may be specific to the polymer formulation, printer architecture, geometry, orientation, and process window studied.
- Some process variables are difficult to randomize independently; temperature, viscosity, speed, and cooling may be physically coupled.
- Whole-plot restrictions reduce effective sample size and power.
- Sensors may not capture local melt, interface, or transient conditions that directly govern bonding.
- Mediator analyses remain vulnerable to unmeasured mediator–outcome confounding, even when treatment is randomized.
- Destructive characterization often requires companion coupons, introducing imperfect correspondence with the tensile specimen.
- Material aging, nozzle wear, and machine drift may be nonlinear or history-dependent.
- Fractional screening designs may alias higher-order interactions; confirmatory experiments are required.
- Standard tensile coupons may not represent structural parts with different geometries, toolpaths, thermal masses, or loading states.
- A residual printer or batch effect identifies unexplained systematic variation but does not itself establish a physical mechanism.
- Failure to detect an effect within the studied range does not show that the factor is universally irrelevant.

